$DIR/main.p:25:10: m was moved to n at line 24, so it can no longer be used here
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so that what
    it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 24, n owns it and m holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If you need two independent values, copy it explicitly at line 24:
        n := m.Clone()
    If n only needs to look at the value, give it a pointer instead:
        n := &m
    If this is the last use of m, move it before line 24, where m still holds
    its value.
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:10: b may have been moved at line 29, so it cannot be used here
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. b is moved into flush (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at
    line 29 on some paths to this point and not on others. On those paths b
    holds nothing afterwards, and the compiler cannot let a value be used where
    it may already belong to someone else.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Use b before line 29, or give it a new value on the path that moves it:
        b = Buf{}
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3302" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:34:10: cannot move t.left out of t: t.left is a field, not a variable
    Buf is an owner type, so taking t.left would move it. But t would still
    believe it owns t.left and would free it again when t is dropped.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To move it out and leave an empty value in its place:
        v := take(&t.left)
    To look at it without moving it:
        v := &t.left
    To copy it:
        v := t.left.Clone()
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3303" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:9: out was moved into flush (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at line 39 in the previous iteration of this loop
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. out is declared outside the loop, and the loop moves its value
    away at line 39 without giving it a new one, so when the next iteration
    starts out holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Declare out inside the loop, so that each iteration has its own:
        for ... {
            var out Buf
            ...
        }
    Or give out a new value after line 39:
        out = Buf{}
    Or move a copy at line 39:
        out.Clone()
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3302" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:43:23: cannot move c inside a function literal: the literal may run more than once, or never
    c is a variable of the function around the literal (declared at main.p:42),
    which the literal uses where it lives. Moving it here would leave c empty
    after the first call, and the function could not tell whether c still needs
    to be dropped.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Pass the value to the literal as an argument:
        do := func(c Buf) { ... }
        do(c)
    (see "Function literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3305" for a longer explanation)
